## Abstract

The Hodge Complex Standard Model (HCSM) derives the gauge structure, matter content, particle spectrum, mixing matrices, spacetime dimension, graviton, and Newton constant of the Standard Model from a single input: the spacetime dimension is four. The dimension itself is derived from the photon's two helicity states and independently from the equal entropy spacing of the torus Laplacian. The substrate is the periodic eight-by-eight torus. On this substrate the Hodge complex of differential forms carries the Hodge-Dirac operator, whose square is exactly the discrete Laplacian on each form degree. The fourteen modes at the self-paired eigenvalue four are identified with one Standard Model generation. This paper establishes the framework's foundational objects, states the notation, and gives a systematic mapping from the prior WIN-era papers to the HCSM paper stack. For each prior paper, we assign one of four verdicts — retained, reframed, sharpened, or retired — and we state which HCSM paper supersedes it. All theorems cited here are HCSM-native: they use only the eight-by-eight torus, the Hodge complex, the D-four action, the discrete Laplacian, the winding generator, and the zero state. No imported machinery — continuum path integrals, SYK kernels, Ryu-Takayanagi strip integrals, Kaluza-Klein towers — is used in any theorem stated as HCSM-native.

