## 2. The substrate

### 2.1 The periodic eight-by-eight torus

The substrate of HCSM is the periodic square lattice with sixty-four sites arranged in an eight-by-eight grid with periodic boundary conditions. Site (x, y) is identified with site (x + 8, y) and with site (x, y + 8); equivalently, the coordinates x and y are each defined modulo eight.

The lattice is not a discretization of an underlying continuum. It is not a finite approximation to a smooth manifold. It is not a mesh on top of a smooth space. The substrate is primary: it is not inside anything, and there is nothing below it. Its sixty-four sites are a structural count, like the number of vertices of a cube, not a spatial extent.

Topologically, the periodic lattice is equivalent to the torus.

### 2.2 The discrete five-point Laplacian

Each site (x, y) has four nearest neighbors: (x plus or minus one, y) and (x, y plus or minus one), with all coordinates modulo eight. The natural differential operator on the lattice is the discrete five-point Laplacian:

> The Laplacian at (x, y) equals four times f(x, y) minus f(x+1, y) minus f(x-1, y) minus f(x, y+1) minus f(x, y-1).

The normalization — four in front of f(x, y) — is chosen so that the constant function is annihilated by the Laplacian.

The eigenvalues of the Laplacian on plane waves are four minus two times the cosine of pi n-one over four, minus two times the cosine of pi n-two over four, where n-one and n-two range over the integers from zero to seven. The spectrum is mirror-symmetric under the transformation that sends lambda to eight minus lambda, with thirteen distinct eigenvalues.

### 2.3 The two independent anchors and the derived third anchor

The substrate size is not put in by hand. It is determined by two independent anchors. A third anchor follows as a theorem.

**Anchor 1 (input): photon helicity.**
A massless spin-one particle has exactly two helicity states. Each is described by a complex amplitude with two real components. Two states times two components equals four real numbers, spanning a four-dimensional phase space whose Wick rotation produces four-dimensional Lorentzian spacetime. Hence the spacetime dimension is four.

**Anchor 2 (input): equal entropy spacing.**
For an L-by-L torus, each mode has a Shannon entropy in bits. The modes fall into entropy classes. Among all integers L from four to ten thousand, the only L for which the torus Laplacian has exactly three equally-spaced entropy classes is L equal to eight. The three classes are six point zero, five point five, and five point zero bits, with spacing of zero point five bits. Hence L equals eight.

**Anchor 3 (derived): topology of the kernel.**
Given the substrate selected by Anchors 1 and 2, the kernel of the Hodge-Dirac operator on the torus has dimension equal to the sum of the Betti numbers, which equals one plus two plus one, which equals four. This is stated and proved in Section 3.5 as a theorem. It is not an independent input; it is a consequence of the substrate selected by Anchors 1 and 2.

The logical dependency graph is therefore:

> Anchor 1 gives the spacetime dimension is four. Anchor 2 gives L is eight. Together, {dimension is four, L is eight} gives N equal to sixty-four, which gives Anchor 3.

The consistency of the spacetime dimension four with L equal to eight follows from the framework relation N equals d times two-to-the-d, which gives N equals sixty-four when d is four, and L equals the square root of N equals eight. Deriving the relation N equals d times two-to-the-d from first principles is the subject of HCSM-01 and is currently listed as a pending item (Section 10). At the level of HCSM-00, we take the relation as a framework identity and note that it is consistent with both anchors.

### 2.4 The fourteen-mode count at the self-paired eigenvalue

The torus has exactly fourteen modes at the self-paired eigenvalue four. The multiplicity equals two times (L minus one), which at L equal to eight gives two times seven equals fourteen. This matches one Standard Model generation. For any other L, the count is wrong. The torus is therefore not merely preferred by the entropy anchor; it is unique.

