## 4. The spectral midpoint and the fourteen-mode multiplet

### 4.1 The self-paired eigenvalue

Solving the mirror symmetry equation gives the self-paired midpoint eigenvalue equal to four. This eigenvalue has multiplicity fourteen and cannot cancel with any mirror partner. The fourteen modes at this eigenvalue are the residue that survives.

### 4.2 The three D-four orbits

The fourteen modes at the eigenvalue four decompose under the D-four point group of the square into three orbits:

**Orbit O-zero:** two modes, support sixty-four.

**Orbit O-one:** eight modes, support forty-eight.

**Orbit O-two:** four modes, support thirty-two.

The support classes are therefore sixty-four, forty-eight, thirty-two, which equal N, V, and N-over-two, with V equal to (d minus one) times two-to-the-d, which at d equal to four gives three times sixteen equals forty-eight.

The inverse participation ratios (IPR) are exactly two over one hundred and twenty-eight, three over one hundred and twenty-eight, and four over one hundred and twenty-eight on the three orbits. The Shannon entropies are exactly six point zero, five point five, and five point zero bits, with equal spacing of zero point five bits.

### 4.3 The Hodge lift to the fifty-six-dimensional sector

The fourteen-fold degeneracy at the eigenvalue four lifts to the full Hodge complex. The lift gives fourteen copies of the zero-form sector, twenty-eight copies of the one-form sector, and fourteen copies of the two-form sector, for a total of fifty-six.

This fifty-six-dimensional sector is the arena of the physical world. Its D-four content decomposes as nine copies of A-one, five copies of A-two, seven copies of B-one, seven copies of B-two, and fourteen copies of E.

