## 9. Retired layers

Several layers from the WIN-era paper stack used imported machinery that is not HCSM-native. These layers are retired from the HCSM paper stack. None of them is cited by any HCSM-native theorem. Each is either replaced by an HCSM-native theorem or is pending an HCSM-native derivation, as stated explicitly below.

**SYK/QIN substrate.**
The Sachdev-Ye-Kitaev Hamiltonian on N Majorana fermions was used in the WIN-era stack to provide a microscopic model for chaotic scrambling. It was removed from the framework in Version 2 of the WIN Paradigm Explained: direct computation of the infrared structure of the one-form fermionic sector establishes that site-local, D-four-invariant quartic interactions do not flow to SYK. The correct infrared description of the one-form sector is a two-plus-one-dimensional local fermionic theory, not SYK. In HCSM, this layer is retired entirely.

**ENTROPIX/MESA.**
The master entropy selection framework was used in the WIN-era stack to select the unique configuration consistent with the master conditions M1 and M2. It is not referenced by any HCSM-native theorem, and the HCSM paper stack does not depend on it. Retired.

**Kaluza-Klein graviton at 1.52 TeV.**
The WIN Gravity Theorem contained a Kaluza-Klein interpretation of the first excited graviton level. This interpretation imported the assumption that the lattice is a compactified spatial dimension, which contradicts the framework's central result that the kernel of the Hodge-Dirac operator has dimension four and that spacetime emerges from the substrate. It is retracted as a category error in HCSM-49. Any detection of a discrete graviton spectrum at accessible scales would falsify HCSM.

**Five through eight-dimensional tower.**
Several WIN-era papers — particularly those on black hole evaporation and the cosmological constant — used a tower of extra dimensions labeled from five-dimensional through eight-dimensional, with an archival persistence layer at the eight-dimensional level. None of these layers is HCSM-native. They are retired.

**Ryu-Takayanagi strip integral.**
The DME stability coefficient was derived in the WIN-era stack from the finite part of the holographic entanglement entropy of a strip via the Ryu-Takayanagi formula. The strip integral is a continuum object. An HCSM-native derivation of the stability coefficient from a discrete computation on the torus is pending (HCSM-55). Until that derivation is complete, the DME layer is treated as pending import removal, and no HCSM theorem depends on it.

**EDF Ohmic bath.**
