This paper has established the foundation of the Hodge Complex Standard Model. The substrate is the periodic eight-by-eight torus, selected by two independent anchors — photon helicity for the spacetime dimension, and equal entropy spacing for the substrate size — and a derived third anchor: the topology of the Hodge-Dirac kernel. The substrate carries the Hodge complex with the Hodge-Dirac operator whose square is exactly the discrete Laplacian on each form degree. The fourteen modes at the self-paired eigenvalue four are identified with one Standard Model generation. The processor T and the class partition are the machine's operation. The output structures — charge, mass, flavor, gravity, cosmology — are the residue after mirror cancellation.

Every theorem cited in this document is HCSM-native. Where prior papers used imported machinery, this document states the verdict clearly. Retired layers are retired; pending layers are named with their specific blocker; writable layers are one hundred percent HCSM-derived.

The complete HCSM paper stack consists of sixty numbered papers. At the time of this writing, thirty-four are writable, twenty-five are pending one open item each, and two are retired. The four critical blockers that unblock the entire remaining stack are: the substrate-size relation; the stability coefficient from HCSM-native computation; the loop pass duration selection; and the mass-map coefficient from higher-order DNLS.

The framework's claim is that the Standard Model is what the substrate looks like when you observe it. The Hodge complex is the machine. Physics is what the machine cannot cancel.

